# mise/bins — executable names for lazy tools whose backend has no registry
# `bins` metadata.
#
# mise can only create a "bootstrap shim" (the stub that installs a lazy tool on
# first use) if it knows the command name. Registry tools (fd, btop, kubectl, …)
# get that for free. Anything declared with an explicit backend — `cargo:`,
# `npm:`, `pypi:`, `conda:`, `ubi:`, `github:` — must state it here, or mise logs:
#
#   mise WARN  Skipping invalid lazy shim declaration: lazy tool cargo:cargo-watch
#              has no registry bin metadata; set lazy_bins explicitly
#
# Format: <package-or-crate>=<exe>[,<exe>…]  (exes are what you type, not crates)
# The key is the entry's unqualified name — the part after the last `/` — so
# `npm:@mermaid-js/mermaid-cli` is keyed as `mermaid-cli`.
#
# Find them for a new entry with:  tar -xO <crate>.crate */Cargo.toml | rg '^\[\[bin\]\]' -A2
# or, for npm:                      npm view <pkg> bin

# --- crates-*.txt: cargo: backend (verified against the crate manifests) ---
